>
	<p>
		Baseball is notoriously known for its unpredictable nature and shifts in momentum that can occur at 
		any moment during games. With this idea we <strong>intend to capture the knowns and discover part of 
		the unknowns.</strong> I'm personally proud of the ability to predict the attendance of guests to ball parks 
		allowing management to prepare and additionally scale their pricing for improved revenue. 
		Additionally it’s an amazing opportunity to map out and deploy code that can be used by the masses. 
		<strong>- Rodolfo, Website Administrator  </strong>
	</p>

	<p>
		Our project is unique because it combines MLB attendance, ballpark characteristics, weather, scheduling, 
		and matchup data into one interactive analytics application. Rather than only displaying baseball statistics, 
		it addresses a real-world business problem by identifying which game conditions may create higher demand for 
		tickets. The project <strong>would be useful to teams for planning promotions, evaluating attendance trends, and 
		supporting ticket-pricing decisions</strong>, while also demonstrating practical database design, query optimization, 
		and web application development.
		<strong>- Kyle, Frontend Development</strong>
	</p>

	<p>
		Some baseball games start with half the stadium still empty. The unsold tickets are revenue that the stadium won’t get 
		back. We hope to assist ticketing departments with the pricing value of tickets based on data that makes one ticket more 
		valuable than another. The factors such as weather, opponent quality, day of the week, and the stadium itself all affect 
		the value of a seat. In our project, we want to create an application that <strong>provides an estimate on the attendance of a game 
		which will correlate with the value of each seat in a stadium.</strong> 

		<strong>- Jimmy, Quality Assurance </strong>
	</p>

	<p>
		Sports when looked at as a business are always on the cutting edge of tech and math, which surprises people because they don't see their 
		favorite team or league as a business. The MLB pulls in over 12 billion dollars every single year, and the reason they manage to do so is 
		based on using algorithms to raise prices of tickets, concessions, merch, parking, etc. as high as they can without decreasing attendance by 
		pricing people out. This idea is only further supported with private equity being allowed to start buying stakes in the ownership of these teams 
		as of 2019 (the first major American professional sports league to permit private equity investment directly into its franchises). <strong>Our project aims 
		to develop something similar to what teams/investors use to try and project some of these stats regarding attendance, and possibly what the price of 
		tickets would look like as well.</strong>
		<strong>- Grant, Data Aquisiton and Deployement</strong>
	</p>

	
</Layout>
